<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Paeonia</h1><p class="st-meta">535 BCE – 217 BCE (approx.) · capital Bylazora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient kingdom of the Axios (Vardar) valley north of Macedon, whose kings minted fine silver coins before falling under Macedonian control.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Paeonians held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> valleys from early times; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> lists them as allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. In 511 BCE the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabazus">Megabazus</a> deported many of them to Asia. A kingdom with its own coinage emerged in the 4th century BCE, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bylazora">Bylazora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stip">Astibos</a> (Štip).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyppeios">Lyppeios</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patraos">Patraos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audoleon">Audoleon</a> issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and horsemen, and Audoleon was honoured at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>. The economy rested on herding, farming and the trade route along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Axios</a>. The Paeonians worshipped the sun and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> called Dyalos, and were famed riders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> forced them into dependency after 358 BCE, and they served in Alexander's army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Celtic invasions in 279 BCE weakened the kingdom. In 217 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bylazora">Bylazora</a>, ending its independence, and after 168 BCE Paeonia became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Roman Macedonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-paeonia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Paeonia" width="320" height="293"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paionia_-_king_Lykkeios_-_359-335_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Zeus_-_Herakles_and_the_lion_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>535 BCE – 217 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bylazora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Agis, Lyppeios, Audoleon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Paeonian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Paeonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>, <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Paeonia, Paeonians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-376&amp;focus=kingdom-of-paeonia" aria-label="Kingdom of Paeonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5216 655 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Paeonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5536 911L5548 987M5536 911L5552 978"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5548 987h0M5552 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5536 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5536 911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-376&amp;focus=kingdom-of-paeonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

